<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Pate Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1862 CE (approx.) · capital Pate</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading city-state of the northern Swahili coast in the 17th and 18th centuries, ruled by the Nabhani dynasty and a cradle of Swahili poetry.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pate-chronicle">Pate Chronicle</a> claims that the Nabhani family, once rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, arrived on Pate island in the 13th century and took power by marriage. Archaeology shows the town flourishing by the 14th century, though the chronicle's early dates are doubted. From Pate the sultans gradually extended their influence over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lamu">Lamu</a> archipelago and the mainland coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pate was a merchant city ruled by a sultan and leading families, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, cloth and slaves. It submitted to the Portuguese for a time but backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> against them. In the 18th century Pate reached its peak, and its scholars produced some of the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> written poetry, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utendi-wa-tambuka">Utendi wa Tambuka</a>, composed in 1728.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Succession quarrels, defeat by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lamu">Lamu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shela">Battle of Shela</a> around 1812 and the rise of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a> brought decline, and Pate came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a>. In 1858 the Nabhani leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-simba">Ahmad Simba</a> moved to the mainland and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/witu-sultanate">Witu Sultanate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1300 CE – 1862 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:11.18%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1862 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pate</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad Simba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pate, Sultanate of Pate, Nabhani Pate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1581&amp;focus=pate-sultanate" aria-label="Pate Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5820 2256 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pate Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6140 2512L4773 1044M6140 2512L6562 1563M6140 2512L6123 2523M6140 2512L6136 2518"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M6562 1563h0M6123 2523h0M6136 2518h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 2512h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 2512h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1581&amp;focus=pate-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
